<h1>Settings</h1>
<p class="lede">Keys this site needs, set from here instead of from a terminal.</p>

<p class="muted" id="settings-denied" hidden>This page is for administrators.
If you are signed in and seeing this, your account is not one — ask an admin on
<a href="/admin/people/">the people page</a>. If you are not signed in,
<a href="/auth/login">sign in</a> first.</p>

<div id="admin-settings" hidden>
  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>A secret is never shown back.</strong> For a masked field this
    page can tell you that a value is set, when, and by whom — never what it is.
    A screen that can show a key back is a screen that leaks every key to anyone
    who reaches it once.</p>
    <p><strong>Plain fields are the ones that are public by nature</strong> — an
    address you could read on the people page anyway, a Google client id that
    appears in every visitor's OAuth redirect. Those show their current value so
    you can edit rather than retype. Masking them would teach you that the
    asterisks elsewhere mean nothing.</p>
    <p>Stored values are encrypted with this instance's <code>SETTINGS_KEY</code>
    and win over anything set at deploy time. Clearing one falls back to the
    deploy-time value if there is one.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="counts" id="settings-unavailable" hidden></p>

  <table class="people"><thead>
    <tr><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th><th>State</th><th></th></tr>
